summary:
- topic: Student Recognition
  text: The board celebrated the South Portland High School Girls' Basketball team
    for winning the state championship and recognized three SPHS students for their
    Scholastic Art Awards.
- topic: FY2026 Budget Presentation
  text: The Superintendent presented the proposed FY2026 budget, which includes a
    5.98% increase in the total school operating budget ($72.9M), requiring a $3.17
    million increase from local taxpayers.
- topic: FY2026 Budget Drivers
  text: Key budget drivers creating a potential $9 million shortfall include the loss
    of COVID-era and other carryover funds, contractual salary increases for three
    bargaining units, and rising health insurance costs.
- topic: Personnel Reductions
  text: The proposed budget includes significant personnel reductions, eliminating
    over 20 full-time equivalent positions, including the Director of Curriculum,
    elementary SEL teachers, ESOL staff, and specialists in music and social studies.
- topic: Administrative Reorganization
  text: The administration plans to reorganize curriculum leadership by distributing
    responsibilities among existing administrators and creating a new Dean of Students
    position at the high school. Library services will become a "special" to cover
    teacher planning time.
- topic: Policy and Program Approvals
  text: The board approved several policies on second read (EGAD, EGAD-R, IJOA) and
    unanimously approved the expansion of the pilot pre-kindergarten program through
    a public-private partnership at no cost to taxpayers.
- topic: Public Comment
  text: Numerous teachers, staff, parents, and students spoke during public comment,
    expressing deep concern over the scale of the cuts, the impact on students (especially
    in SEL, special education, ESOL, and arts), and the lack of transparency and collaboration
    in the budget process.
- topic: Superintendent's Response
  text: The Superintendent responded to some questions, promising more data at upcoming
    workshops, and defended the budget as a difficult but necessary balance of district
    needs and taxpayer impact.
